<%# What just happened, said once, to the readers who cannot see it happen. #336.

    This screen changes without a page load -- Done, Undo, a rating, a correction, and the
    background poll that notices an assistant's write all swap markup in place. A sighted
    lifter sees the row turn green, the bar advance and the count go from 2 to 3 of 12.
    Everyone else was told nothing at all, and the only way to find out whether a tap had
    registered was to navigate back into the row and read it -- one-handed, mid-set, which
    is the moment this app exists for. WCAG 4.1.3 is about exactly that.

    role="status" carries an implicit aria-live="polite", and both are written out because
    support for the pair is better than for either alone. Polite rather than assertive: it
    waits for a pause instead of cutting across whatever is being read, which is what a
    confirmation should do. A confirmation that interrupted the row you were reading would
    be its own bug.

    sr-only rather than hidden. A region with display:none is not announced at all, so the
    sentence has to be in the layout and merely unpainted.

    Empty on the first render, because nothing has happened yet -- and it has to be on the
    page from the start regardless: a live region announces changes to itself, so one that
    arrives already carrying its sentence is a region that says nothing. Every response
    that changes this screen sends it back with hx-swap-oob, which costs no extra request.

    Outside #lift-panels, like the poller above it, or the poll's innerHTML swap would take
    the region away and the next announcement would have nowhere to land. %>
<div id="session-announcement"<%== ' hx-swap-oob="true"' if defined?(oob) && oob %>
     role="status" aria-live="polite" class="sr-only"><%= defined?(message) ? message : '' %></div>
